      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Ocean Shores sits on the Point Brown Peninsula, a 6,000‑acre stretch 6 miles long and 2 miles wide along Grays Harbor.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.historylink.org/File/23141",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The first white settler on the peninsula was Matthew McGee, who arrived in the early 1860s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ocean_Shores%2C_Washington",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1960, the Ocean Shores Development Corporation bought the peninsula for $1 million, sparking a rapid resort‑style boom.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ocean_Shores%2C_Washington",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The city’s early growth included a 1963 marina opening, 100 motel units, and a six‑hole golf course, all built on sandy ground.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
